(r/'qa/COHORT-REVIEW.md').write_text('''# FX11: observed phase and seam review\n\n## Primary wind\n\nThe original source identity is retained: broad ochre gust ribbons, small earthy flecks and dry wind-blown tufts. The ribbons are individually redrawn with changing contours and fragmentation, not shifted copies or fades.\n\n1. Curl A has one broad S-shaped sweep. The measured interior crest is around source X 347, Y 482.\n2. Curl B advances the main crest right to X 612, Y 505. A left incoming hook is already visible near X 180. The lower wisp moves from the left region into the central region.\n3. Breakup has a separated outgoing ribbon at X 783–1236 and a substantial following curl at X 133–795. The following curl crest is near X 385. The lower wisp continues to the right.\n4. Renewal advances that following crest into the middle at X 651 while another left incoming crest is visible at X 307. A narrow outgoing tip remains at X 1103–1242. The final frame remains full and active rather than tapering to dust.\n\nAt 4→1 the left incoming crest advances from approximately X 307 to X 347 and becomes the broader left crest. The rightward central wave deforms into the outgoing low arc of phase 1. This is an interpretation of hand-authored wind deformation; it does not identify exact conserved material points. The topology changes from connected to separated and back. The native seam must be reviewed as a whole, rather than inferring motion from hash differences.\n\n## Secondary details and caveats\n\nSmall dust flecks change shape and position, but some linger. No claim is made that every fleck follows a continuous monotonic trajectory; the directional read comes primarily from the advancing ribbon cohorts and lower wisps. The final frame's lower-left wisp is renewal, not the old rightward wisp jumping back.\n\nThe two tufts are intended as stationary contacts. Their measured lower edges vary by at most 0.69 logical pixels on the left and 0.87 on the right. At the wrap they move upward by 0.65 and 0.87 logical pixels respectively. This is a disclosed residual source-drawing variation, not corrected with pixel shifting.\n\nThe total source alpha mass changes by under 1.8%, which argues against a large volume flash but is not motion evidence. Full and 3→4→1→2 seam sheets have been inspected at native 32/64 and zoom on black, white, bright stone, green ground, dark earth and checker. No backing card, clipping or visible exterior glow was found. Parent static-phase acceptance was received on 2026-10-20 after full and seam native review.\n\nExact APNG timing and decoded-frame equality are separately verified. Live/browser observation of eight uninterrupted cycles has not occurred because of the established environment blocker. No live game integration is claimed.\n''')
